Yeah. The useful move is probably not to chase every mechanism at once, but to keep the core invariant:

organisms are continuously remodeled by the demands they repeatedly encounter.

Then for any trait you care about, ask four things: what layer carries it, what inputs reliably push that layer, how long that layer takes to adapt, and what the failure mode is if you overdo it.

That turns a huge biological idea into something operational. You stop asking “Can I rewild myself?” and start asking, “What environment would make this capability increasingly necessary, safe, and automatic?”